},{"affno":"1","first":"Mads Holm","key":"christensenmadsholmpau","last":"Christensen","locid":"9155958a-da5a-4ee7-835b-634a1d7a4cab","name":"Christensen, Mads Holm","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Ida","key":"thaarupidacpau","last":"Thaarup","locid":"2e3fd9f1-db06-4012-868d-ecdcdb96d8f2","name":"Thaarup, Ida C.","name_org":"Thaarup, Ida","orcid":"0000-0001-9004-0978","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 1","first":"Thomas","key":"bjarnsholtthomaspau","last":"Bjarnsholt","locid":"cb3ccb7d-0415-40e5-9bba-c9bc26b6316d","name":"Bjarnsholt, Thomas","orcid":"0000-0002-8003-7414","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Karen L.","key":"demesybentleykarenlpau","last":"de Mesy Bentley","name":"de Mesy Bentley, Karen L.","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","first":"Andreas","key":"petersenandreaspau","last":"Petersen","name":"Petersen, Andreas","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Henrik Elvang","key":"jensenhenrikelvangpau","last":"Jensen","locid":"20245e41-1eb4-4cfa-a2d6-e6851e101300","name":"Jensen, Henrik Elvang","orcid":"0000-0001-7277-7829","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Louise Kruse","key":"jensenlouisekrusepau","last":"Jensen","locid":"1edba00c-b457-4660-85a3-24c07c4919f6","name":"Jensen, Louise Kruse","rank":13,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["40470607"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/apm.70031","issn":["09034641","0903465X"],"issn_org":["09034641"],"issue":"6","journal":"APMIS","paperid":"e70031","status":"p","title":"APMIS","volume":"133"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105008296833"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Governmental Institutions","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-GV-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Governmental Institutions","Hospitals","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-2a4e3acf-9c02-4765-a3b6-3bffd2f127af"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Delayed Bacterial Neutrophil Recruitment and Bacterial Bone Dispersion : New Identified Factors in Peri-Prosthetic Joint Infection Development. Insights From an Adult Minipig Model","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-818771","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Is it time to rethink the inoculum of animal models of implant-associated infections (IAI)? Traditionally, animal models of IAI are based on inoculation with metabolically active overnight cultures of planktonic bacteria or pre-grown surface-attached biofilms. However, such inoculums do not mimic the clinical initiation of IAI. Therefore, the present study aimed to develop a clinically relevant inoculum of low metabolic micro-aggregated bacteria. The porcine Staphylococcus aureus strain S54F9 was cultured in Tryptone Soya Broth (TSB) for seven days to facilitate the formation of low metabolic micro-aggregates. Subsequently, the aggregated culture underwent filtration using cell strainers of different pore sizes to separate micro-aggregates. Light microscopy was used to evaluate the aggregate formation and size in the different fractions, while isothermal microcalorimetry was used to disclose a low metabolic activity. The micro-aggregate fraction obtained with filter size 5–15 μm (actual measured mean size 32 μm) was used as inoculum in a porcine implant-associated osteomyelitis (IAO) model and compared to a standard overnight planktonic inoculum and a sham inoculum of 0.9 % saline. The micro-aggregate and planktonic inoculums caused IAO with the re-isolation of S. aureus from soft tissues, bones, and implants. However, compared to their planktonic counterpart, neither of the micro-aggregate inoculated animals showed signs of osteomyelitis, i.e., sequester, osteolysis, and pus at gross inspection. Furthermore, inoculation with low metabolic micro-aggregates resulted in a strong healing response with pronounced osteoid formation, comparable to sham animals. In conclusion, the formation and separation of low metabolic bacterial micro-aggregates into various size fractions is possible, however, planktonic bacteria were still seen in all size fractions. Inoculation with micro-aggregates caused a less-aggressive osteomyelitis i.e. combination of infected tissue and strong healing response. Therefore, the use of low metabolic micro-aggregates could be a relevant inoculum for animal models of less-aggressive and thereby slower developing IAI and add in to our understanding of the host-implant-bacteria interactions in slow-onset low-grade infections.","author_count":12,"contributors":["Hartmann, Katrine Top","Lund Nielsen, Regitze","Mikkelsen, Freja Cecilie","Aalbæk, Bent","Lichtenberg, Mads","Jakobsen, Tim Holm","Bjarnsholt, Thomas","Kvich, Lasse","Ingmer, Hanne","Odgaard, Anders","Jensen, Henrik Elvang","Jensen, Louise Kruse"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-777135"],"filters":{"added":"2024-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Animal model","Bacterial aggregates","Biofilm","Infection"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"bacterial micro aggregates as inoculum in animal models of implant associated infections","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bioflm.2024.100200","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-777135","identifier":[{"id":"38803605","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85193075960","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC11128829","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["ku-1f20906f-5aff-4b99-909b-c2b46ee7223c"],"keyword":["Animal model","Bacterial aggregates","Biofilm","Implant-related infections","Infection"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-826BCBFBD668142C1D07102AFF0413BB"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/410171246/Bacterial_micro_aggregates.htm","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bioflm.2024.100200","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/392579220/Bacterial_micro_aggregates.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Veterinary and Animal Sciences, Pathobiological Sciences"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital - Rigshospitalet"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Katrine","key":"tophartmannpau","last":"Top Hartmann","locid":"24872909-edcd-41d3-ac0e-6d89e6fef67b","name":"Hartmann, Katrine Top","name_org":"Top Hartmann, Katrine","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Regitze","key":"lundnielsenpau","last":"Lund Nielsen","name":"Lund Nielsen, Regitze","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Freja Cecilie","key":"mikkelsenpau","last":"Mikkelsen","locid":"2e12d6c8-0973-4aad-bb54-cdfbbdf0fa54","name":"Mikkelsen, Freja Cecilie","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Bent","key":"aalbækpau","last":"Aalbæk","locid":"dc92f1f0-60dd-4ce6-978c-1e9465720689","name":"Aalbæk, Bent","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Mads","key":"lichtenbergpau","last":"Lichtenberg","locid":"879963fc-f554-43eb-8062-b5c35c0dcc38","name":"Lichtenberg, Mads","orcid":"0000-0002-0675-4554","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Tim","key":"holmjakobsenpau","last":"Holm Jakobsen","locid":"c4df6233-6aa5-4ff3-b95f-68f445643741","name":"Jakobsen, Tim Holm","name_org":"Holm Jakobsen, Tim","orcid":"0000-0002-1671-2155","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","first":"Thomas","key":"bjarnsholtpau","last":"Bjarnsholt","locid":"cb3ccb7d-0415-40e5-9bba-c9bc26b6316d","name":"Bjarnsholt, Thomas","orcid":"0000-0002-8003-7414","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Lasse","key":"kvichpau","last":"Kvich","locid":"b0087d9e-6ad9-4c48-837c-f949923e67c9","name":"Kvich, Lasse","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Hanne","key":"ingmerpau","last":"Ingmer","locid":"abe2cd31-84e7-4f06-81a3-33e08bf93b8f","name":"Ingmer, Hanne","orcid":"0000-0002-8350-5631","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 1","first":"Anders","key":"odgaardpau","last":"Odgaard","locid":"33eeba1f-407c-4aa4-bf69-5efb12863f82","name":"Odgaard, Anders","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Henrik","key":"elvangjensenpau","last":"Elvang Jensen","locid":"20245e41-1eb4-4cfa-a2d6-e6851e101300","name":"Jensen, Henrik Elvang","name_org":"Elvang Jensen, Henrik","orcid":"0000-0001-7277-7829","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Louise","key":"krusejensenpau","last":"Kruse Jensen","locid":"1edba00c-b457-4660-85a3-24c07c4919f6","name":"Jensen, Louise Kruse","name_org":"Kruse Jensen, Louise","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["38803605"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.bioflm.2024.100200","issn":["25902075"],"journal":"Biofilm","paperid":"100200","status":"p","title":"Biofilm","volume":"7"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85193075960"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-1f20906f-5aff-4b99-909b-c2b46ee7223c"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Bacterial micro-aggregates as inoculum in animal models of implant-associated infections","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-777135","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Aim: To make an inoculum for induction of Implant-Associated Osteomyelitis (IAO) in pigs based on bacterial aggregates resembling those found on the human skin, i.e. aggregates of 5-15 µm with low metabolic activity. The aggregates were evaluated and compared to a standard planktonic bacterial inoculum. Method: The porcine Staphylococcus aureus strain S54F9 was cultured in Tryptone Soya Broth for seven days. Subsequently, the culture was filtered through cell strainers with pore sizes of 15 µm and 5 µm, respectively. The fraction of 5-15 µm aggregates in the top of the 5 µm filter was collected as the aggregate-inoculum. The separation of aggregates into different size fractions was evaluated by light microscopy. The metabolism of the aggregate-inoculum and a standard overnight planktonic inoculum was evaluated with isothermal microcalorimetry.In total, six female minipigs were allocated into three groups (n=2), receiving different inoculums. Group A: overnight planktonic inoculum; 104 CFU S. aureus (S54F9), Group B: seven days old 5-15 µm aggregate-inoculum; 104 CFU S. aureus (S54F9), Group C: saline. All inoculums were placed in a pre-drilled implant cavity in the right tibia of the pig and a sterile stainless-steel implant was inserted. The pigs were euthanized seven days after surgery. Postmortem macroscopic pathology, microbiology, computed tomography and histopathology were performed. Results: The separation of aggregates into different size fractions was done successfully by the filtering method. Isothermal microcalorimetry showed, a delayed Time-to-peak metabolic activity of the aggregate-inoculum compared to the planktonic inoculum. S. aureus was isolated from subcutis, bone and implants from all animals in groups A and B. Both group A animals showed osteomyelitis at gross inspection with suppuration and sequestration, while groups B and C animals had no macroscopic lesions. From CT scans, both group A animals also showed positive signs of osteomyelitis, i.e., osteolysis, while only one animal in group B did, and none in group C. Histopathological examination of the bones showed more extensive inflammation in group A animals compared to those in group B, which showed more osteoid formation. Conclusions: Formation and separation of low metabolism bacterial aggregates into different size fractions was possible. The aggregates can be used as inoculum in the porcine IAO model, with microbiological re-isolation from both implants and tissue. Furthermore, the aggregates caused a less aggressive IAO, than the planktonic counterparts. Using aggregated bacteria as inoculum appears to be more relevant to the clinical situation of infecting bacteria.","author_count":11,"contributors":["Hartmann, Katrine Top","Nielsen, Regitze Lund","Mikkelsen, Freja Cecilie","Ingmer, Hanne","Kvich, Lasse","Aalbæk, Bent","Odgaard, Anders","Jensen, Henrik Elvang","Lichtenberg, Mads","Bjarnsholt, Thomas","Jensen, Louise Kruse"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-702108"],"event":{"end":"2023-10-14","place":"Basel, ch","start":"2023-10-12","title":"EBJIS 2023","year":"2023"},"filters":{"added":"2023-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"rethinking the inoculum used in animal models of implant associated osteomyelitis the formation and application of bacterial aggregates","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/370802474/EBJIS2023_programmebook_final.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-702108","ids":["ku-a3439dd9-20ad-434c-bb61-4d4ea9b77521"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Local-1-1-2